
Local-only: one `System` in one process. Excluded as a class: remote and cluster watch (#443), `addressTerminated`, remote deployment, cluster receptionist and sharded pubsub, durable delivery queue implementations, `io` over real sockets (replaced by `net`), Java/Scala APIs, JVM executors and JFR. This must be stated in the README of every affected module so the result cannot be read as complete Apache Pekko parity.

**Accepted statement (ADR 0033 D7, 2026-10-24).** "100%" means coverage of the local capabilities included in the inventory matrix, with the distributed-systems exclusions and the JVM-only exclusions enumerated explicitly. It is not total parity with Apache Pekko, which includes cluster and remoting. Every module README and every closure report must say so.

## 8. Parity judge

### 8.1 What exists

The judge checks one JSON event log per run against named clauses (`docs/judge.md:11-23`; `judge/comparator.py` `EVENTS`, `PARAMS`, `RULES`). Tiers: G (documented guarantee), I (verified in source), S (goPekko-only spec), N (never asserted). Modes: controlled (CallingThreadDispatcher + ExplicitlyTriggeredScheduler on Pekko, injected executor and clock on Go) and concurrent (300 runs minimum, calibrated N only for S19/GMstop and S21/GMoverlap). 22 scenarios, 15 in `judge/go-scope.json`; Pekko mutations M1-M67, Go mutations GM*. Both sides use the same `comparator.py` and `evaluate.py`. The Pekko harness `judge/pekko-harness/Judge.scala` (572 lines) is classic-only; the Go harness is `pekko-actor/actor/judge_internal_test.go` (1,184 lines, an internal test). It asserts nothing about stash, timers, FSM, routing, scheduler, dispatchers, event stream, io, serialization or Typed.

### 8.2 What Typed requires (design only, no execution)

1. A second Pekko harness (`JudgeTyped.scala`) with its own `runMain`, `judge.sbt` also depending on `actor-typed` and `actor-testkit-typed` (project names to be confirmed in `build.sbt`), and a harness-name argument in `run-pekko.sh` (it hard-codes `Judge.scala`).
2. Event recording through `Behaviors.receiveSignal` and `setup`. There is no `PreStart` hook in Typed (record at `setup`); no `post-restart` signal (a restart re-runs `setup`); no decider callback, so the `decided` event cannot be written as in Classic (assert lifecycle outcomes instead, as G7 does).
3. New event kinds, each in all three comparator tables: `signal`, `timer-started/cancelled/fired` (key, generation), `stashed/unstashed`, `adapted`, `registered/listing`, `routed`, `published/topic-delivered`, `delivery-ack`, `spawned-via`.
4. New relation rows, scenarios (numbered after S22) and mutation rows in `migration/stage-1/milestone-1.md`, since `scripts/check-judge-matrix.py` fails on a relation without a scenario and a mutation.
5. A Go typed surface to drive (needs the Behavior layer to exist). Judge design can proceed before; judge execution cannot.

### 8.3 Candidate scenario families and mutations

| Family | Oracle | Candidate mutation |
|---|---|---|
| Typed default Stop on failure | `Behaviors.supervise` source | default restarts instead of stops |
| Signals ordering vs messages (`PreRestart`, `PostStop`, `Terminated`, `ChildFailed`) | typed source, docs | skip `PostStop` signal; signal before in-flight message ends |
| Timers with ManualTime: fire, cancel, generation filter | `TimerScheduler` | timer fires after cancel; late timer message not filtered |
| Stash: replay order, capacity, behavior change | `StashBuffer` | replay out of order; capacity ignored |
| Message adapter and `context.ask`: failure, timeout, exactly-once | `ActorContext` | adapter applied twice; ask completes twice |
| Receptionist (local): register, find, subscribe, deregister on stop | `LocalReceptionist` | no deregistration on stop |
| Routers: round-robin, routee termination (random is N-tier) | `PoolRouterImpl`, `RoutingLogic` | stopped routee still routed |
| `Topic`: subscribe, publish, unsubscribe on stop | `TopicImpl` | delivers to stopped subscriber |
| Delivery controllers (local): ordering, ack, redelivery, flow control | `delivery/*` | skip an ack |
| `SpawnProtocol` | `SpawnProtocol.scala` | name collision accepted |

Random routing, timer concurrency and delivery redelivery are timing-dependent; assert only relations that hold for every allowed order.

For Classic additions (stash, timers, scheduler, `PoisonPill`, `become`, receive timeout, routing, circuit breaker, backoff) the same extension applies to `Judge.scala`.

### 8.4 Inventory plumbing

`docs/parity/capabilities.json` is `inventory_status: "bootstrap"` with `"capabilities": []` and only persistence adapters; `-strict` and `-closing` exit 1 on it, and only Slice A of `docs/parity/proposal.md` is integrated (B to E not started). Extending the M/O/E and L0-L4 schema and the `pekko-persistence-*` module discovery to actors is **unapproved design work**. Until the Owner approves it, this document's section 4 is the working inventory, kept as prose. The manifest has no field for judge scenario or spec references (the proposal defers it to Slice C).

### 8.5 Design versus execution

Design (documents, specs, matrix rows, inventory schema): can start now. Execution (writing `JudgeTyped.scala`, Go typed scenarios, running sbt or `go test`, calibration, mutation reports): needs an authorized story and the Behavior layer for the Go side. The repository's checkers already treat unexecuted work as `written` or `designed`.

